      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">The opening line — your most important sentence</h2>
      <p>LinkedIn shows only the first ~220 characters of your About section before truncating to "see more." The opening line must establish your professional identity immediately — before anyone decides whether to click.</p>
      <h3>Weak openings (common patterns)</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>"I am a passionate professional who loves solving problems."</li>
        <li>"Experienced software engineer with a track record of success."</li>
        <li>"I believe that great teams build great products."</li>
        <li>"Results-driven leader with 10+ years of experience."</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These waste the first line. They say nothing specific and fail the hook entirely.</p>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">Strong openings</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>"Staff Software Engineer specializing in distributed systems and platform infrastructure at Series B–public company scale."</li>
        <li>"Senior Product Manager with 7 years building B2B SaaS products — currently at [Company] owning a $40M ARR product line."</li>
        <li>"VP of Engineering with a background in cloud-native infrastructure and a track record scaling engineering orgs from 15 to 80+."</li>
        <li>"Senior FP&A Manager | $400M ARR SaaS | Board-ready financial storytelling | CFA."</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Building the body — outcomes and specialization</h2>
      <p>After the opening identity statement, the body of your About section should cover two things: what you have accomplished (with numbers) and what you specifically do best.</p>
      <h3>Outcomes — what to include</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Your 2–3 most impressive, relevant results — not a full resume recap</li>
        <li>Quantify every outcome: "$12M ARR shipped," "40% latency reduction," "scaled team from 8 to 35 engineers"</li>
        <li>Connect outcomes to business impact, not just task completion</li>
        <li>Use the format: built/led/shipped/reduced/grew [X] → resulting in [Y]</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">Specialization — what problems you solve</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Name your specific domain: "real-time data pipelines," "growth and monetization," "compliance infrastructure"</li>
        <li>Describe the type of problem you are best at: "I specialize in scaling systems that break between Series B and IPO"</li>
        <li>Mention industries or company types if relevant: "B2B SaaS, fintech, marketplace businesses"</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

